How to Move Data from On-Prem to Cloud & Its Dependency on Self-Hosted vs. Auto-Hosted Cloud 

1. Moving Data from On-Prem to Cloud 

The approach depends on factors like data volume, latency, frequency, security, and cost. Here are common methods: 

✅ Small to Medium Data Volumes 

Direct Upload (SCP, SFTP, API, SDKs) → Push data to cloud storage (AWS S3, Azure Blob, GCS). 

Database Migration Service (DMS) → Use AWS DMS, Azure DMS, or GCP DMS for database migration. 

✅ Large Data Volumes (TBs to PBs) 

Cloud Transfer Appliances → AWS Snowball, Azure Data Box, GCP Transfer Appliance. 

Parallel / Multipart Uploads → Chunking large files and uploading in parallel. 

✅ Real-Time / Streaming Data 

Message Queues / Streaming Platforms → Kafka, AWS Kinesis, Azure Event Hubs, GCP Pub/Sub. 

Apache NiFi → Continuous data ingestion pipelines. 

✅ Hybrid Sync & Continuous Data Movement 

Storage Gateways → AWS Storage Gateway, Azure File Sync, GCP Filestore. 

Direct Connections → AWS Direct Connect, Azure ExpressRoute, GCP Interconnect. 

✅ ETL & Data Integration 

Cloud-Native ETL Tools → AWS Glue, Azure Data Factory, GCP Dataflow. 

Third-Party Tools → Talend, Fivetran, Informatica, Matillion. 

 
 

2. Impact of Self-Hosted vs. Auto-Hosted Cloud on Data Migration 

Factor 

Self-Hosted Cloud (VMs, Kubernetes, etc.) 

Auto-Hosted Cloud (Managed Services) 

Data Transfer Complexity 

You handle network configurations, storage setup, and transfer optimizations. 

Managed services handle networking, storage, and migration. 

Scalability 

Requires manual resource allocation and scaling. 

Auto-scales based on workload. 

Security & Compliance 

You manage security, encryption, and access policies. 

Provider handles encryption, access control, and compliance. 

Latency & Performance 

Dependent on infrastructure setup and tuning. 

Optimized by cloud provider. 

Cost 

Can be cheaper but requires admin effort. 

Pay-as-you-go, but may be costlier in the long run. 

Key Considerations 

Auto-Hosted Cloud (e.g., AWS RDS, Google BigQuery, Azure Synapse) is easier to migrate to because providers offer built-in migration tools. 

Self-Hosted Cloud (e.g., running PostgreSQL on an EC2 instance) gives flexibility but requires manual migration and setup. 

Conclusion 

If you are moving to a managed service (Auto-Hosted Cloud), use DMS, cloud-native ETL tools, or direct file transfers for easy migration. 

If you are moving to a Self-Hosted setup, you might need custom scripts, storage gateways, and manual configurations for data transfer. 

 

   

