Scheduling a Pipeline to Run on the Last Day or Last Working Day of the Month 

You can schedule a pipeline to run on the last day of the month or the last working day of the month using various methods depending on the tool you are using (Airflow, Azure Data Factory, Apache NiFi, etc.). 

 
 

1. Scheduling for the Last Day of the Month 

Using CRON Expression 
If the tool supports cron scheduling (e.g., Airflow, Azure Data Factory, Apache Airflow, etc.), use: 

bash 

CopyEdit 

0 0 28-31 * * [ "$(date +\%d -d tomorrow)" == "01" ] && your_script.sh  

This ensures the script only runs when the next day is the 1st of the next month. 

Example Cron Schedules: 

Airflow DAG Schedule: 0 0 28-31 * * (runs daily but only executes logic when tomorrow is the 1st) 

Azure Data Factory CRON: 0 0 28-31 * * with an expression to check if it's the last day. 

Apache NiFi: Use ExecuteScript or InvokeHTTP to check the last day before execution. 

 
 

2. Scheduling for the Last Working Day of the Month 

Using Python Logic in Airflow 

python 

CopyEdit 

from datetime import datetime, timedelta import pandas as pd def is_last_working_day(execution_date): next_day = execution_date + timedelta(days=1) last_business_day = pd.date_range( execution_date.replace(day=1), execution_date, freq='BM' # Business Month End )[-1] return execution_date == last_business_day if is_last_working_day(datetime.today()): print("Run the pipeline")  

Implementation in Airflow DAG: 

python 

CopyEdit 

from airflow import DAG from airflow.operators.python import PythonOperator from datetime import datetime def check_and_run(): if is_last_working_day(datetime.today()): print("Pipeline running...") dag = DAG( "last_working_day_pipeline", schedule_interval="0 0 28-31 * *", start_date=datetime(2024, 1, 1), catchup=False, ) run_task = PythonOperator( task_id="run_if_last_working_day", python_callable=check_and_run, dag=dag, )  

 
 

3. Tumbling Window vs. Scheduled Window in Streaming Pipelines 

Trigger Type 

Tumbling Window 

Sliding Window 

Definition 

Fixed-size, non-overlapping time intervals. 

Overlapping time intervals that slide over time. 

Example Use Case 

Batch processing every 1 hour, 1 day, etc. 

Real-time analytics where data should not be missed. 

Overlap 

No overlap between windows. 

Windows overlap, so some data may be included in multiple windows. 

Best For 

Aggregations (e.g., daily sales total). 

Real-time alerting and anomaly detection. 

Use Cases in Azure Data Factory or AWS Kinesis 

Tumbling Window Trigger: Use when you want the pipeline to run exactly at a fixed time interval (e.g., last day of the month). 

Sliding Window Trigger: Use when you want to process overlapping data chunks (e.g., last 24 hours of data every 6 hours). 

 
 

Conclusion 

Use CRON Jobs for scheduling based on date conditions. 

Use Python logic in Airflow or SQL queries to determine the last working day. 

Use Tumbling Windows for fixed interval batch jobs. 

Use Sliding Windows for real-time overlapping data processing. 

         

