3️⃣ Flatten Nested JSON Columns 

Since Spark reads nested fields as struct and arrays, we need to flatten the data. 

from pyspark.sql.functions import col, explode 

  

# Flatten Nested Structure 

df_flat = df.select( 

    col("id"), 

    col("name"), 

    col("address.city").alias("city"), 

    col("address.street").alias("street"), 

    col("address.zip").alias("zip"), 

    explode(col("orders")).alias("order") 

).select( 

    col("id"), 

    col("name"), 

    col("city"), 

    col("street"), 

    col("zip"), 

    col("order.order_id").alias("order_id"), 

    col("order.amount").alias("amount") 

) 

  

df_flat.show(truncate=False) 

 
 

4️⃣ Handling More Complex JSON Scenarios 

Scenario 

Solution 

Deeply Nested Fields 

Use col("field.subfield") to extract. 

Arrays Inside Arrays 

Use explode() multiple times. 

JSON with Dynamic Schema 

Use inferSchema=True or from_json() for custom parsing. 

Handling Missing Fields 

Use df.fillna("default_value"). 

 
 

